<h3 align="center">Type Annotation Study</h3>

<!-- GETTING STARTED -->
## Getting Started

### Prerequisites
* Python 3.10
* Poetry 1.5.1
* CodeQL CLI 2.16.2

### Installation
1. Unzip/Clone the repo
2. Move to the repository's directory
```sh
    cd typeconfusion_study
```
3. Install Python depencies via PyPoetry
   ```sh
   poetry install
   ```

<p align="right">(<a href="#readme-top">back to top</a>)</p>



<!-- USAGE EXAMPLES -->
## Usage
The CLI tool gives access to each phase of our approach via individual commands.

A list of commands for the CLI and documentation about their usage can be accessed with the following command:
```sh
poetry run python3 typeconfusion_study/main.py --help
```

Further documentation about each command and its arguments can be accessed via:
```sh
poetry run python3 typeconfusion_study/main.py [COMMAND] -h
```

More verbose output for all commands is available by setting the `-d` flag:
```sh
poetry run python3 typeconfusion_study/main.py -d [COMMAND]
```
### Sampling
We porvide the samples we use for our study in the data/ directory. To use different sample, replace these samples with your own samples obtained from GHS. Make sure the names are identical and to format matches that of the existing samples.

The following command automatically clones the NUMBER first repositories in the SAMPLE sample (javascript/typescript/python) and generates their codeql databases:

```sh
poetry run python3 typeconfusion_study/main.py sample -s SAMPLE -n NUMBER
```

Additionally, the `-r` flag may be set to refresh the sample, i.e., delete existing databases and start over.

#### Example command to generate the JavaScript dataset:
```sh
poetry run python3 typeconfusion_study/main.py sample -s javascript -n 10000 -r
```

Note that the script may occasionally get stuck if a repository is no longer available and as for GitHub credentials. In that case, the script will timeout after 10 minutes and resume with the next repository. Alternatively, the script can be terminated (`Ctrl-C`) and will skip the package on consecutive iterations unless the `-r` flag is set.

### Static Analysis
After sampling projects for a given language, run the following command to statically analyze the sample:
```sh
poetry run python3 typeconfusion_study/main.py analyze -s SAMPLE -o OUTPUT
```

SAMPLE must again be one of javascript/typescript/python, and OUTPUT must be the path to a .csv file.
#### Example command to analyze the JS dataset:
```sh
poetry run python3 typeconfusion_study/main.py analyze -s javascript -o results/javascript_05_06_24.csv -v
```

### Callgraph Construction
The CLI features a command to generate the callgraph for individual repositories. The script clones the repository, generates the CodeQL database and creates the callgraph:

```sh
poetry run python3 typeconfusion_study/main.py callgraph SAMPLE FULL_NAME -db DATABASE_DIR -d
```

FULL_NAME refers to the full name of a repository, i.e., {organization/repository}. Note that the callgraph construction is only supported for JavaScript and TypeScript projects. The `-db` flag is optional and allows passing the path to the repositories CodeQL database if was already generated previously to avoid cloning the repository. 

The script generates both the raw `.dot` files and plots the graphs in PDF format. The outputs are stored under `results/callgraphs/<REPOSITORY_NAME>`.

### Evaluation
To evaluate static analysis results, run:
```sh
poetry run python3 typeconfusion_study/main.py evaluate JS_CSV TS_CSV PY_CSV
```
The evaluation results are saved to `results/evaluation.json` and plots are stored in reNote that evaluation requires results for all three languages.

#### Example command to evaluate the analysis results provided in this artifact:
```sh
poetry run python3 typeconfusion_study/main.py evaluate results/javascript_05_06_24.csv results/typescript_03_06_24.csv results/python_31_05_24.csv
```

## Troubleshooting
### Poetry prending dependency installs
In case poetry gets stuck during the installation of dependencies, please refer to [this](https://github.com/python-poetry/poetry/issues/6050#issuecomment-1522626132) as a potential solution.

